1
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:05,510
Good morning everyone.

2
00:00:05,760 --> 00:00:08,910
Most kitchen gas alarms use one sensor and one threshold.

3
00:00:09,340 --> 00:00:14,210
They go off when someone boils water or fries food, so people switch them off.

4
00:00:14,340 --> 00:00:16,910
Then nobody is protected when a real leak happens.

5
00:00:17,080 --> 00:00:19,560
This project is an LPG leak detector that can

6
00:00:19,600 --> 00:00:22,330
tell the difference between a leak and normal cooking.

7
00:00:22,740 --> 00:00:26,870
The detector is built on an STM32F401 Black Pill.

8
00:00:27,200 --> 00:00:31,570
Three metal-oxide gas sensors feed the ADC through voltage dividers:

9
00:00:31,860 --> 00:00:39,440
an MQ-6 for LPG, an MQ-2 for smoke, and an MQ-135 for alcohol and VOCs.

10
00:00:39,480 --> 00:00:42,770
An AHT20 gives temperature and humidity.

11
00:00:43,160 --> 00:00:49,020
A small OLED shows the status, a buzzer and an LED raise the alarm,

12
00:00:49,060 --> 00:00:52,550
and an HC-05 module streams data to a phone over Bluetooth.

13
00:00:53,380 --> 00:00:57,500
The firmware is register-level C with CMSIS, no HAL,

14
00:00:57,540 --> 00:01:02,610
and it fits in about nineteen kilobytes of flash.

15
00:01:05,280 --> 00:01:06,180
First, smoke.

16
00:01:06,480 --> 00:01:09,170
An incense stick about twenty centimetres from the sensors.

17
00:01:09,440 --> 00:01:11,750
MQ-2 reacts first and drops the most.

18
00:01:11,960 --> 00:01:13,910
The detector labels the air SMOKE.

19
00:01:14,220 --> 00:01:18,910
On the alarm check page the vote boxes stay empty, and the buzzer stays silent.

20
00:01:19,260 --> 00:01:21,630
A normal gas alarm would already be sounding.

21
00:01:22,940 --> 00:01:24,090
Now real LPG.

22
00:01:24,400 --> 00:01:27,170
An unlit lighter, gas button pressed for two seconds,

23
00:01:27,420 --> 00:01:29,070
ten centimetres from the sensors.

24
00:01:29,540 --> 00:01:34,710
MQ-6 drops hardest, the tree says LPG, and the vote boxes fill one by one.

25
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:39,390
At six boxes the buzzer starts and the screen flashes GAS LEAK.

26
00:01:39,700 --> 00:01:42,450
In this run the alarm came seconds after the gas.

27
00:01:42,760 --> 00:01:44,080
Once the air is clean again,

28
00:01:44,120 --> 00:01:47,530
the alarm clears by itself after five seconds of safe readings.

29
00:01:47,880 --> 00:01:49,650
Now the mobile app.

30
00:01:49,860 --> 00:01:52,380
The phone app runs in Chrome over Bluetooth using Web Serial,

31
00:01:52,420 --> 00:01:54,410
served from an https page.

32
00:01:54,620 --> 00:02:00,120
It shows the same status live: the air class, the model's votes, the MQ-6 bar,

33
00:02:00,160 --> 00:02:03,960
gas levels, temperature, humidity and a two-minute chart.

34
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:06,760
It beeps on an alarm, sends commands to the board,

35
00:02:06,800 --> 00:02:09,310
and records labelled data for training.

36
00:02:10,740 --> 00:02:11,640
To sum up.

37
00:02:11,680 --> 00:02:14,240
An ordinary gas alarm cannot tell a leak from cooking,

38
00:02:14,280 --> 00:02:17,610
so it is either annoying or switched off.

39
00:02:17,880 --> 00:02:20,280
By reading three gas sensors and humidity together,

40
00:02:20,320 --> 00:02:23,920
and running a small decision tree on the microcontroller itself,

41
00:02:23,960 --> 00:02:26,760
this detector stayed quiet through smoke and steam,

42
00:02:26,800 --> 00:02:29,810
and raised the alarm within seconds of real LPG.

43
00:02:30,280 --> 00:02:33,850
We chose a decision tree because it is the right size for this chip.

44
00:02:34,120 --> 00:02:37,130
A depth-five tree is at most thirty-one comparisons,

45
00:02:37,480 --> 00:02:40,740
so it needs no neural network library, runs in microseconds,

46
00:02:40,780 --> 00:02:43,830
and takes only a few hundred bytes of flash.

47
00:02:44,420 --> 00:02:48,490
It is also explainable: every branch is a plain rule you can read,

48
00:02:48,720 --> 00:02:53,490
for example that LPG pulls MQ-6 down much harder than MQ-135,

49
00:02:54,660 --> 00:02:56,730
while smoke hits MQ-2 first.

50
00:02:57,040 --> 00:03:00,220
The tree is trained on a laptop with scikit-learn from our own recordings,

51
00:03:00,260 --> 00:03:01,460
exported as C code,

52
00:03:01,500 --> 00:03:03,600
and we checked that the C version gives the

53
00:03:03,640 --> 00:03:06,420
same answer as the Python model on every sample.

54
00:03:06,460 --> 00:03:12,440
The whole system runs on a 16 megahertz core in about nineteen kilobytes of flash,

55
00:03:12,480 --> 00:03:16,980
using low-cost, off-the-shelf parts, and the training pipeline is open,

56
00:03:17,020 --> 00:03:19,570
so anyone can record their own data and improve the model.

57
00:03:19,980 --> 00:03:22,740
The next steps are testing with calibrated gas concentrations,

58
00:03:22,780 --> 00:03:25,160
collecting data over more days and more kitchens,

59
00:03:25,200 --> 00:03:28,070
and building an enclosure for long-term use.

60
00:03:28,160 --> 00:03:29,300
Thank you for watching.
